percu ccsbrief_mdg_fr hazel clothier hampson epdf 842x tb00846 alegana okiro','Abstract Background In Madagascar, a sentinel surveillance system was set up in 18 hospitals since 2014 and was managed by the Ministry of Public Health and the Institut Pasteur de Madagascar. In order to improve the access to appropriate health care in Madagascar, the main clinical diagnoses in hospitalized patients were analyzed. Methods At hospitalization of a patient, each unit involved in the sentinel surveillance recorded the clinical diagnosis through an e-health platform. Data from September 2014 to July 2018 were analyzed. Morbidity and annual incidence of diseases according to ICD-10 were reported. Results A total of 140,789 inpatients information was recorded. The median age was 28.2 years (IQR: 18.3; 45.3). 21.6% of the children < 15 years suffered from communicable diseases. The hospital morbidity was 4.01% for malaria, 0.84% for tuberculosis, 0.09% for HIV/AIDS and 0.05% for plague. The hospital morbidity of non-communicable diseases was higher compared to communicable diseases with 7.8%, 7.1% and 3.1% for ”Diseases of the circulatory system”, "Injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes" and "Mental and behavioural disorders”, respectively. "Pregnancy, childbirth and puerperium" represented 20.5% of the hospitalized patients. Conclusions The e-health platform enabled continuous and standardized data collection. Communicable diseases affect mainly children < 15 years. Non-communicable diseases are on the rise and need more attention by national health authorities. A number of hospitalizations could be prevented by a better health care management at the community-based health care level and by implementation of a Universal Health Coverage (UHC) in Madagascar. Keywords: Madagascar, Hospital sentinel network, Web-based database, e-Health platform, Communicable diseases, Non-communicable diseases Introduction Madagascar is the world second largest island and located about 400 km (250 miles) of the coast from East Africa. Madagascar has a low-income economy with the estimated gross domestic product (GDP) per capita of 501.76 US dollars in 2020 and 71.7% of the population living below the poverty line [ 1 ]. Access to healthcare remains insufficient for many Malagasy, especially in rural areas. Hospitals, medical centers and dispensaries are mainly found in urban areas with community-based health centers (« Centre de Santé de Base”; CSB) available at community level. The CSB are serviced by a doctor or more commonly a nurse, midwife or a Community Health Worker (“Agent Communautaire”; CHW) [ 2 ]. Low-income countries are usually associated with communicable diseases such as malaria, tuberculosis, and rabies; in addition, Madagascar is still facing outbreaks of bubonic and pneumonic plague [ 3 ]. However, recent health indicators indicate an increasing prevalence of non-communicable diseases as many other low-income countries [ 2 , 4 ]. In Madagascar, all health facilities are required to use a paper-based health information system since more than 30 years and to monthly report the diagnosis and the basic demographic data of all patients to the Ministry of Public Health (MoH), which enters the data into an electronic central database. However, the data quality originating from the majority of health facilities, especially in remote rural areas, remains poor in terms of completeness and timeliness [ 5 ]. Given the data quality issue, the interpretation remains difficult, i.e . the decrease of malaria cases could be due to a true decrease of malaria or due to non-reporting. However, the availability of health indicators in space and time constitutes an asset in terms of health research and a tool to support decision-making process for national health authorities [ 6 , 7 ]. Knowledge of the main hospitalized patients’ diagnoses would help clinicians and other health care workers and subsequently would improve the management of patients. Furthermore, a more accurate diagnosis and intervention at the level of community-based health centers may reduce the hospitalizations. In addition, timely recognition of disease outbreaks reduces its spread and its potential lethal consequence. Therefore, the MoH with its longtime partner “Institut Pasteur de Madagascar” (IPM) initially implemented a fever sentinel surveillance network in 2007 to identify in real time diseases with epidemic potential, which had been reported on a weekly basis by short message service (SMS) (“Mobile Health” platform) [ 8 ]. In 2014, with the progress of the information and communication technology, the sentinel hospital network further moved to an e-health platform and implemented a web-based form to collect medical and demographic information from hospitalized patients. The anonymized data were immediately transferred to the web-based database and centralized at IPM. This database was shared with the MoH. In Madagascar, the access to health care greatly varies by region and urbanization (higher concentration of health care facilities in urban compared to rural areas), by material resources (in most areas, the public health infrastructure, such as sanitation, medical supplies, electricity, and water supply are inadequate) and by diseases (disease control of some diseases receives more financial support from foreign donors). Further, most of the people do not have a health insurance. In this context, part of the hospital admissions is a consequence of this lack of access to community-based care. The main clinical diagnoses in hospitalized patients in Madagascar through data collected by a sentinel hospital network were analyzed with 3 main objectives: To estimate the proportion of communicable vs non-communicable diseases in hospitalized patients; To describe the diagnoses by population characteristics (age, sex); To evaluate the data completeness collected by the web-based (case by case reporting) sentinel hospital network “e-health” and to compare it to the monthly activity report (aggregated data reporting) health information system. Methods Hospital Sentinel Network and Study Population In 2014, a sentinel hospital network of 18 hospitals out of 198 hospitals in Madagascar was set up (“e-health”platform) by the IPM and the MoH. These were composed of seven university-based hospitals (“Centre Hospitalier Universitaire”, CHU), seven regional reference hospitals (“Centre Hospitalier de Référence Régionale”, CHRR) and four district reference hospitals (“Centre Hospitalier de Référence du District”, CHRD). The choice of these sentinel hospitals was based on three criteria: (a) representativeness of all five bio-climates in Madagascar such as (i) per humid in the East coast, (ii) humid in the margin highlands, (iii) mountain in the central highlands (iv) sub humid in the Western coast and (v) semi arid in the South (Fig. 1 ) [ 9 ], (b) hospitals, which were not specialized and hence represented a large stratum of the population based on gender and age, and (c) areas with different economic activities, such as mining, tourism and livestock trading. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Geographical location of the 18 sentinel hospital, Madagascar, 2014–2018. CHRD: District reference hospitals. CHRR: Regional reference hospitals. CHU: University-based hospitals The 18 sentinel hospitals were located in 14.9% (17/114) of health districts in Madagascar. Of the 18 sentinel hospitals, seven were located in the margin highlands, five in the East coast, four in the Western coast and two in the South. The population catchment areas of these sentinel hospitals represented 21.3% of the Malagasy population (5,463,618 / 25,674,196). While two sentinel university-based hospitals, CHU Toamasina and CHU Befelatanana, were located in an area of high population density (> 10,000 people/km 2 ), six sentinel hospitals were situated in an area of low population density (< 100 people/km 2 ). The source population was the population in the health district catchment areas and the study population constituted of patients admitted to and hospitalized in the hospital sentinel for medical (non-surgical) reasons. Data Collection and Standardization (ICD-10 Codes) At entry of the patient, each unit at the sentinel hospital used a paper-based standard register to enter socio-demographic information of the newly hospitalized patient, the clinical diagnosis made by a doctor, a nurse or a midwife, the reason for hospitalization and laboratory diagnostic test results. Inpatients were included in the study if admitted to a sentinel hospital for a minimum of 24 h. For communicable diseases, laboratory diagnostic tests and data on diagnostic test results were only available for malaria, plague, tuberculosis (TB) and HIV/AIDS. Malaria was confirmed by blood smear examination (microscopy) or rapid diagnostic test. The malaria rapid diagnostic test detected all species of Plasmodium spp . using antigens against Plasmodium falciparum histidine-rich protein-2 (PfHPR2) and parasite lactate dehydrogenase (pLDH) [ 10 ]. The parasitemia value and the species identified were not registered into the data base. The TB test was performed by sputum smear microscopy to identify Mycobacterium tuberculosis (confirmatory test). A plague rapid diagnostic test using a sample of pus contained in the buboes (swellings), or in the sputum of persons with suspected pneumonic plague was used to detect the antigen F1 (F1RDT) which is part of the outer surface of Yersinia pestis [ 11 ]. A positive plague rapid test can identify a probable case and requires a confirmatory test result. HIV/AIDS was tested to detect a probable case with rapid diagnostic tests in whole blood or oral fluid specimens with two serial of tests: the first test was designed to detect IgM antibodies for HIV by enzyme immunoassays (EIAs) and the second test was an additional assay like Western blot to isolate the viral antigen (p24 capsid) by radioimmunoassays (RIAs) [ 12 ]. These four diseases were diagnosed based on the case definitions given by the MoH, using results from clinical examination and laboratory tests. Unfortunately, no standardized clinical algorithms were used for the clinical diagnosis of other diseases. At each sentinel hospital, a data clerk entered the anonymous inpatient information from the paper register into a login- and password-secured web-based database using Django, a high-level Python web framework. For every new hospital admission, a new record with a unique identifier number was created. The database was kept on a secured server at the Epidemiology and Clinical Research Unit of IPM and was regularly and automatically updated. The clinical diagnosis and laboratory test results were recorded into the database in short or rich text format. In order to statistically analyze the clinical diagnosis, it required to be standardized. We hence classified the diagnoses and hospitalization reasons by applying the International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems, 10th revision (ICD-10) system [ 13 ]. First, a sample of the data was coded manually to establish an automatic text classification algorithm. Once the algorithm was set up, the text was assigned automatically to an ICD-10 class by using Python 2.7 using the Django web framework. The ICD-10 transcoding was first done by group, then by block and sub-block and went up to the disease level for some records, depending on the available information. Data Analysis We used Excel version 2010 (Microsoft) and R version 3.2.1 ( www.r-project.org ) for statistical analysis and QGIS version 3.4.0 ( www.qgis.org ) was applied for maps. Proportions were used to describe the hospital and patient characteristics. For each variable and category, (i) age group, (ii) gender, (iii) pregnancy status and (iv) geographical location of the sentinel hospital were described. For quantitative data, the median and interquartile range (IQR) were measured (due to missing data, the median was a better measure than the mean). The Pearson’s Chi2 test was used to determine a statistically significant difference (p-value ≤ 0.05) between group categories. To measure the annual incidence of a disease occurring in hospitalized patients, we calculated the number of cases in hospitalized patients (for example malaria) divided by the number of persons living in the districts served by the hospital per year. The morbidity among the hospitalized patients was calculated by dividing the diagnosed cases by the total number of hospitalized inpatients of the sentinel hospitals within the study period. The annual hospital attendance percentage was measured by dividing the annual recorded inpatients by the number of residents of the district for each hospital. Furthermore, we analyzed the distribution of sentinel hospitals by number of districts, population density and hospital level. The source population was calculated by dividing the number of persons from a district served by the sentinel hospital by the total population of Madagascar during the study period. To assess the quality of ICD-10 data transcoding, we estimated the proportion of reasons for hospitalization appropriately and correctly coded among all those coded by the macro-mean and micro-mean of the F-measures [ 14 ]. The performance score varied by zero to 1. Data Quality (Completeness) We compared the number of records registered by the newly implemented web-based sentinel surveillance system (“e-Health” platform) with the number of cases record routinely and monthly reported by the Health Information System (HIS) from January 2015 to July 2018. The difference between these two systems or “completeness” was expressed as a proportion (%), where the aggregated monthly activity reported by hospital surveillance system was used as the baseline. Internal and External Validity The internal validity was maintained by using a standard paper register of hospitalized patients, refreshing training for all agents to record inpatient information, and by making available the standard operating procedures (SOP) to surveillance team members. The external validity/ representativeness of the sentinel surveillance was evaluated by: a) assessing the proportion of participating hospitals, by dividing the number of sentinel hospitals by the total number of existing hospitals in Madagascar, and b) by assessing the proportion of participating hospitalized patients divided by the total number of hospitalized patients in Madagascar during the study period. Further, we described the distribution of sentinel hospitals by region, number of districts, population density and hospital level. Ethical Clearance All methods were carried out in accordance with relevant guidelines and regulations for research with anonymous surveillance data of the Ethical Review Committee of Biomedical Research in Madagascar. The use of the data collected anonymously in patients hospitalized in these hospitals participating in sentinel surveillance was exempted from ethical review of human subject research by the Ethical Review Committee of Biomedical Research in Madagascar, and the need for consent to participate was waived (letter N° 111MSANP/SG/AMM/CERBM). Results Hospital Characteristics From September 2014 to July 2018, a total of 140,789 records were entered into the “e-Health” platform with a median 5,603 per hospital (IQR: 3,133; 11,996) and range from 2229 to 19,704 records. The median of the annual hospital attendance percentage was 0.6% (IQR: 0.4; 0.9), so 0.6% of the population living in the catchment areas were hospitalized in the sentinel hospitals during the study period. The median of data completeness was—38.9% (IQR: −12.5; + 51.4) (Table 1 ); hence, overall the web-based platform (case by case reporting) had 38.9% less entries than the routine HIS (aggregated data reporting surveillance system). Table 1. Characteristics of the 18 sentinel hospitals in Madagascar from September 2014 to July 2018 Hospital Health district Level Geographical location Source population Population density (Persons/Km 2 ) Inpatients recorded by Web (case by case reporting) Inpatients recorded by routine HIS (aggregated data reporting) Annual hospital attendance percentage (%) Completeness (% web records / HIS records) Ambatondrazaka Ambatondrazaka CHRR Margin Highlands 358,164 64.7 12,634 3,057 0.9 + 73.6 Antsirabe Antsirabe I CHRR Margin Highlands 246,354 1854.6 17,017 46,921 1.7 −69.5 Befelatanana Antananarivo Renivohitra CHU Margin Highlands 1,274,225 15,000.4 15,225 57,291 0.3 −74.2 Fenoarivo Antananarivo Atsimondrano CHU Margin Highlands 648,780 1538.6 3,092 1,889 0.9 + 37.2 Itaosy Antananarivo Atsimondrano CHRD Margin Highlands 648,780 1538.6 19,704 17,990 0.9 + 8.7 Fianarantsoa Fianarantsoa I CHU Margin Highlands 191,776 2229.6 10,083 20,670 1.3 −55.9 Moramanga Moramanga CHRD Margin Highlands 350,724 49.1 4,527 7,681 0.3 −51.4 Manakara Manakara CHRR East coast 385,860 121.9 4,012 2,139 0.3 + 39.6 Sainte Marie Sainte Marie CHRD East coast 26,822 144.7 2,229 2,617 2.1 −18.1 Sambava Sambava CHRR East coast 397,880 85.0 7,184 6,278 0.5 + 11.1 Taolagnaro Taolagnaro CHRR East coast 340,739 57.2 5,915 6,354 0.4 −16.9 Toamasina Toamasina I CHU East coast 325,857 10,535.2 6,264 964 0.5 + 82.2 Antsiranana Antsiranana I CHU Western coast 129,320 1800.4 3,256 3,601 0.6 −17.3 Mahajanga Mahajanga I CHU Western coast 246,022 4822.2 5,290 0 0.5 NA Maintirano Maintirano CHRR Western coast 113,542 7.6 2,336 2,611 0.5 −18.3 Nosy Be Nosy Be CHRD Western coast 108,266 338.6 2,976 4,177 0.7 −33.1 Morondava Morondava CHRR South 150,531 26.5 16,206 15,883 2.7 −2.0 Toliara Toliara I CHU South 168,756 5257.8 2,839 13,103 0.4 −79.0 TOTAL 5,463,618 140,789 213,226 0.6 − 38.9 Open in a new tab CHU: university hospitals (Centre Hospitalier Universitaire), CHRR: regional reference hospitals (Centre Hospitalier de Référence Régionale), CHRD: district hospitals (Centre Hospitalier de Référence du District); HIS: Health Information System; Annual hospital attendance rate is obtained by dividing the annual inpatients recorded by web by the number of the source population (residents of the district); Completeness was the proportion of records registered by the web-based sentinel surveillance system (“e-Health”) with the number of records routinely and monthly reported by paper by the Health Information System; NA: not available Paper or routine Health Information System was the baseline. + number means that the web-based system has more entries, -number means that the paper based system has more entries Inpatient Characteristics Of 140,789 records, 462 did not have comprehensible entries (numbers instead of text, unknown abbreviations) and were excluded. A total of 140,327 records were automatically classified into ICD-10 groups and blocks. The sex ratio male / female was 0.66 (55,588/84,739). The pregnant women represented 20.5% of the inpatients. The median age for admission was 28.2 years (IQR: 18.3; 45.3). Individuals aged ≥ 15 years represented 81.7% (114,596/140,327) of the inpatients. Most inpatients were recorded in the central highland (46.2%), 26.5% of the inpatients were recorded in the north and western regions, 25.3% in the eastern region and 2.0% in the south. The median axillary temperature was 37.0 °C (IQR: 36.4; 37.5). Almost one third (29.3%) of the inpatients was referred to the sentinel hospitals by a community-based health center (“CSB”) and 70.7% (99,212/140,327) of the inpatients came from the community directly. The median time of the hospital stay was 2 days (IQR: 1; 4). The main reasons for being discharged from the hospital were: 41.8% did not have a vital disorder and continued the health care management at home, 15.1% were referred to a higher level of health care management and 2.8% (3,892/140,327) deceased (3,036 for non-communicable diseases and 856 for communicable diseases). Frequency of Inpatient Diagnoses Based on ICD-10 Proportion of Non-Communicable Diseases in Hospitalized Patients The proportion of the non-communicable diseases was lower in the age group < 15 years than ≥ 15 years (4.6% vs 19.4%, p < 0.01), n = 140,327. I00-I99 “Diseases of the circulatory system” and S00-T98 “Injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes” were the most frequent with 7.8% and 7.1% of the inpatients, respectively. The annual incidence per 100,000 people varied between 4.4 (C00-D48 “Neoplasms”) and 50.0 (I00-I99 “Diseases of the circulatory system”). The prevalence for “Injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes” was 45.7 per 100,000 people. By age group, E00-E90 “Endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases” was higher in age group < 15 years than ≥ 15 years (24.1 vs 7.8 per 100,000 people ( p < 0.01). By gender, V01-Y98 “External causes of morbidity and mortality” and F00-F99 "Mental and behavioural disorders” were higher in males than females with a sex ratio M/F of 1.67, 1.21 and 1.19 respectively ( p < 0.01) (Table 2 ). Table 2. Frequency and annual incidence for chosen non-communicable diseases by age and gender in 18 sentinel hospitals in Madagascar, 2014–2018 Total Age groups p Gender p Sex ratio M/F < 15 years ≥ 15 years Female Male Population 5,463,618 1,344,050 4,119,568 2,809,589 2,654,029 N 140,327 25,635 114,596 84,739 55,588 0.66 Classification C00-D48 “Neoplasms” N 972 96 876 658 314 0.48 Frequency (%) 0.7 0.4 0.8 < 0.01 0.8 0.6 0.94 Inc 4.4 1.8 5.3 < 0.01 5.9 3.0 < 0.01 E00-E90 “Endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases” N 2,577 1,296 1,281 1,289 1,288 1.00 Frequency (%) 1.8 5.1 1.1 < 0.01 1.5 2.3 0.15 Inc 11.8 24.1 7.8 < 0.01 11.5 12.1 < 0.01 F00-F99 “Mental and behavioural disorders” N 4,289 400 3,889 1,958 2,331 1.19 Frequency (%) 3.1 1.6 3.4 < 0.01 2.3 4.2 < 0.01 Inc 19.6 7.4 23.6 < 0.01 17.4 22.0 < 0.01 I00-I99 “Diseases of the circulatory system” N 10,920 1,518 11,828 6,496 4,42