, I00-I99 “Diseases of the circulatory system” and Z00-Z99 “Factors influencing health status and contact with health services” were more frequent in adolescents/adults aged ≥ 15 years than children aged < 15 years (p < 0.01). Communicable diseases described in A00-B99 “Certain infectious and parasitic diseases” were more frequent in the South with semi-arid bioclimate than other regions (p < 0.01) (Table 4 ). Table 4. Inpatients characteristics of the main diagnoses based on ICD-10, 18 sentinel hospitals, Madagascar, 2014–2018 Classification “Certain infectious and parasitic diseases” Diseases of the digestive system” “Diseases of the circulatory system” “Injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes” “Factors influencing health status and contact with health services” Total N 16,387 9,450 10,920 9,981 9,482 140,327 (%) (11.7) (6.7) (7.8) (7.1) (6.8) (100.0) Gender Male 8,685 4,831 4,424 6,236 1,171 55,588 (%) (15.6) (8.7) (8.0) (11.2) (2.1) (100.0) Female 7,702 4,619 6,496 3,745 8,311 84,739 (%) (9.1) (5.5) (7.7) (4.4) (9.8) (100.0) p value < 0.01 < 0.01 0.04 < 0.01 < 0.01 Age groups < 15 years 5,543 2,257 1,518 2,730 152 25,635 (%) (21.6) (8.8) (5.9) (10.6) (0.6) (100.0) ≥ 15 years 8,915 7,193 11,828 7,251 9,330 114,596 (%) (7.8) (6.3) (10.3) (6.3) (8.1) (100.0) p value < 0.01 < 0.01 < 0.01 < 0.01 < 0.01 Geographical location Western coast 6,580 3,818 3,339 4,088 1,861 37,154 (%) (17.7) (10.3) (9.0) (11.0) (5.0) (100.0) Margin highlands 3,992 2,703 4,642 2,538 6,269 64,891 (%) (6.2) (4.2) (7.2) (3.9) (9.7) (100.0) East coast 4,767 2,840 2,659 3,097 1,350 35,467 (%) (13.4) (8.0) (7.5) (8.7) (3.8) (100.0) South 1,048 89 280 258 2 2,815 (%) (37.2) (3.2) (9.9) (9.2) (0.1) (100.0) p value < 0.01 < 0.01 < 0.01 < 0.01 < 0.01 Open in a new tab A00-B99: “Certain infectious and parasitic diseases “; K00-K93: “Diseases of the digestive system”; I00-I99: “Diseases of the circulatory system”; S00-T98: “Injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes”; Z00-Z99: “Factors influencing health status and contact with health services” Pregnant Women Pregnant women represented 20.5% (28,796/140,327) of the inpatients admitted to sentinel hospitals. The main reasons for hospitalizations were O80-O84 “Delivery” (5.4%), Z30-Z39 “Persons encountering health services in circumstances related to reproduction” (5.2%) and O60-O75 “Complications of labour and delivery” (1.2%) (Table 5 ). Table 5. Proportion of ICD-10 code in hospitalized pregnant women admitted to sentinel hospitals in Madagascar, 2014–2018 ICD-10 code N Proportion by pregnant women (%) n = 28,796 Proportion by inpatient (%) n = 140,327 O80-O84 Delivery 7,522 26.1 5.4 Z30-Z39 Persons encountering health services in circumstances related to reproduction 7,245 25.2 5.2 O60-O75 Complications of labour and delivery 1,700 5.9 1.2 I95-I99 Other and unspecified disorders of the circulatory system 1,553 5.4 1.1 O00-O08 Pregnancy with abortive outcome 1,145 4.0 0.8 O30-O48 Maternal care related to the fetus and amniotic cavity and possible delivery problems 928 3.2 0.7 I10-I15 Hypertensive diseases 683 2.4 0.5 O20-O29 Other maternal disorders predominantly related to pregnancy 563 2.0 0.4 R50-R69 General symptoms and signs 549 1.9 0.4 P00-P04 Fetus and newborn affected by maternal factors and by complications of pregnancy, labour and delivery 283 1.0 0.2 O10-O16 Oedema, proteinuria and hypertensive disorders in pregnancy, childbirth and the puerperium 214 0.7 0.2 P90-P96 Other disorders originating in the perinatal period 134 0.5 0.1 B50-B54 Malaria 102 0,4 0.1 O85-O92 Complications predominantly related to the puerperium 13 0.05 0.01 Total 28,796 100 20.5 Open in a new tab Proportion by pregnant women was the percent of cases by dividing the cases number by the total of pregnant women; Proportion by inpatient was the percent of cases by dividing the cases number by the total of inpatients Quality of ICD-10 Data Transcoding In general, the application of the automated algorithm allowed the assignment of ICD-10 codes for the two variables “reasons for hospitalization” and “inpatient diagnosis”. Of the 140,789 records, only 1.7% were not ICD-10 coded because of content errors (in numbers or dates) or unrecognized abbreviations. The number of ICD-10 codes assigned to each record varied from one to seven, but the majority, 80.5% of records, had only one code assigned. With term substitution, the macro-mean and micro-mean of the F-measures were respectively 73.1% and 80.4% regardless of the used classifiers and also the number of characters or words. The average performance score was 0.301, 38.3% of the records had a score of zero and 12.2% of the records had a score > 0.8. Evaluation of the Data Quality In Madagascar, there are 197 hospital centers and the sentinel hospitals represented 9.1% (18/197). From January 2015 to July 2018, a total of 130,283 inpatients were recorded through our web page system and 213,226 inpatients were reported through the routine paper Health Information System (HIS). The median of the completeness was—38.9% (IQR: −12.5; + 51.4), meaning that overall more patients were recorded by the routine aggregated HIS data than the web based system (individual data). However, six sentinel hospitals had registered more records by web form than by the routine HIS. For example, in the sentinel hospital at the CHU Toamasina, there were five times more inpatients recorded by web form than by the routine HIS (6,264 vs 964). Eleven sentinel hospitals had less inpatients recorded by the web page system, than the routine HIS. For the sentinel hospital in Toliara, the data completeness was 21.0%, hence, this hospital had 5 times less records in the web-based system than in the routine HIS (2,938 vs 13,103). On the other hand, the hospital in Mahajanga had no data registered by the routine HIS. The median hospital attendance was 0.6% (IQR: 0.4 – 0.9). The hospital attendance was highest in four sentinel hospitals: Morondava (2.7%), Sainte Marie (2.1%), Antsirabe (1.7%) and Fianarantsoa (1.3%) (Table 1 ). Discussion We describe the first web-based sentinel hospital surveillance (“e-Health” platform) in Madagascar, implemented in 18 hospitals, which collected individual patient information at the time of admission (socio-demographic information, clinical and laboratory diagnosis). The diagnosis was standardized by ICD-10 codes with a total of 140,789 records being entered into the “e-Health” platform from September 2014 to July 2018. While most current national public health programs target infectious diseases, the hospital morbidity of non-communicable diseases was higher compared to communicable diseases, apart for the age group < 15 years of age. I00-I99 “Diseases of the circulatory system” and S00-T98 "Injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes" were the most frequent with 7.8% and 7.1% of the inpatients being assigned to these groups, respectively. Unfortunately, it was impossible to compare these numbers with those of former years, as we could not find any unpublished or published data. However, our hypothesis is that non-communicable diseases in hospitalized patients are emerging as a public health concern in Madagascar. Possible reasons may be greater survival into adulthood, decreases in communicable diseases, urbanization, exposure to environmental pollution, highly processed food intake and insufficient physical exercise. Like in other sub-Saharan African countries, non-communicable diseases in Madagascar seem on the rise [ 2 , 15 – 17 ]. Based on our results, the proportion of patients assigned to I00-I99 “Diseases of the circulatory system” was twice the inpatients with malaria (7.8% vs 4.0%). Therefore, it is important to also focus on the prevention and control of these diseases, by sensitizing the population, actors in the public health sector and policy makers. Mental health problems are accounting for 13% of the total global burden of disease. The burden estimate for low-income countries is 3.2% [ 18 ]. In the sentinel hospital network, there were 4,289 cases classified into F00-F99 “Mental and behavioural disorders” diagnosis. Thus, mental health represented 3.1% of hospitalized patients and the annual incidence was 26.16 per 100,000 people. Mental health conditions frequently create tremendous suffering for the patient and his/her family if not treated and leads individuals and families into poverty, hindering economic development at the national level. In Madagascar like other low-income countries, mental health would need to be recognized as a public health problem and to be included into broader health policies and strategies such as non-communicable diseases or maternal health. Since two decades, various efforts are made to fight morbidity and mortality related to human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, tuberculosis, and malaria. We found the following annual incidence of diseases: 4.01% for malaria, 0.84% for tuberculosis, 0.09% for HIV/AIDS and 0.05 for plague (suspected or probable cases). The described annual incidence of the four communicable diseases does not reflect the true annual incidence in Madagascar, as the majority of malaria, HIV and TB cases are managed at the health based centers, where the global control program is implemented. Hence, our numbers only reflect the incidence of malaria, HIV and TB and plague in hospitalized patients and are not interpretable for the entire population. Despite widespread control and elimination measures, confirmed malaria was the most frequent communicable disease diagnosis in hospitalized patients in Madagascar. It could be useful to evaluate why 4% of malaria cases end up in the hospital, despite the control program at the lower health centers. The data indicates the need to continue to fight against this parasite at community and national levels. The burden of malaria is evaluated by the annual trends of the total malaria cases, the malaria case incidence rate (i.e. the number of cases per 100,000 at-risk people) and malaria mortality rate (deaths per 100,000 at risk people) [ 19 ]. There was no annual data available to identify the trends of malaria cases, which have been hospitalized; hence it is difficult to compare our data. Madagascar aims to eliminate the parasite nationwide, and it is therefore necessary to identify all malaria cases, especially in those populations who are not easy to reach by the national malaria control program. Through the sentinel hospital network, the annual incidence of malaria in hospitalized patients was 25.77 per 100,000 people. The annual incidence was much higher in the age group of children aged 5 to 14 years (45.33 per 100,000 people) and in the Western regions (110.99 per 100,000 people) [ 20 ]. Therefore, it is crucial to extend malaria prevention measures to all age groups (so far it concentrates on children ≤ 5 years), especially at the community-based health center level and to reinforce control strategies [ 21 ]. Tuberculosis (TB) is remaining worldwide one of the major communicable diseases and health concern in the world [ 22 ]. We found that 5.39 inpatients per 100,000 persons suffered from TB. The TB incidence rate was estimated at 233 cases per 100,000 persons in 2019 in Madagascar [ 22 ]. Most of TB patients in Madagascar are treated in dedicated TB treatment centres and not only in hospitals. In hospitals, only severe cases with complications are treated. These complications could be linked to multidrug resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB), which has an estimated prevalence between 3.5% and 18.0% among notified tuberculosis cases worldwide [ 23 , 24 ]. In Madagascar, there were 3,646 (95%CI: 2,258–5,437) cases of presumptive MDR-TB in 2017 (Knoblauch AM et al., 2020) [ 25 ]. MDR-TB is lower in Madagascar than in other countries where TB is endemic; however, national surveys and prevention programs need to be in place to prevent an increase of MDR-TB cases [ 26 ]. In Madagascar, the sex ratio M/F was 1.7 and the frequency increased proportionately with age, which is consistent with the data in the World Health Organization (WHO)’s global surveillance report [ 24 ]. To prevent TB, not only the disease needs to be treated to reduce spread, but also risk factors such as co-morbidities (i.e. HIV/AIDS), smoking and alcohol consumption need to be reduced. There were 69 cases of plague hospitalized during the period of this surveillance study, leading to an annual incidence of 0.32 per 100,000 people. These numbers are lower than the annual incidence (29.58 per 100,000 people) in the at-risk areas, as most cases are detected and treated in outbreak investigations and control programs lead by the MoH at community level [ 27 ]. Madagascar is one of three countries in the world where plague is still endemic and the period of high transmission is generally from August to April in at-risk areas. Plague control programs in those areas ensure that patients infected with Yersinia pestis receive appropriate treatment, care, and hygiene advice to prevent plague from further spreading. In Madagascar, a recent literature review on HIV and AIDS prevalence estimated a prevalence of 0.3% in the general population, with higher prevalences among key populations (Raberahona M et al., 2021) [ 28 ]. In the sentinel hospital study, there were 126 patients diagnosed with HIV/AIDS leading to an annual incidence of HIV/AIDS of 0.58 per 100,000 people. So, more precise epidemiological data and improved HIV/AIDS diagnosis should be a public health priority for a informed HIV/AIDS response. O00-O99 "Pregnancy, childbirth and puerperium" represented 11.3% of the sentinel hospital attendance with a high frequency of the sub-blocks O80-O84 “Delivery”, O60-O75”Complications of labour and delivery “, and O30-O48 “Pregnancy with abortive outcome“. This situation reflects the absence of sufficient support in the diagnosis and treatment of reproductive diseases at the community-based health center level and a need to apply the norms and procedures of “safe motherhood program” as well as the promotion of “adolescent sexual and reproductive health” [ 29 , 30 ]. An e-health platform enabled harmonized and continuous data collection and the near real time analysis of patient data at the facility, regional and national levels. The classification of diseases based on ICD-10 codes enables better comparison of data. The 18 sentinel hospitals were located in 14.9% of all (18/112) districts in Madagascar and these hospitals served 21.3% of the Malagasy catchment population. It was considered sufficiently representative of the population to describe the distribution of diseases in the population in different regions of Madagascar [ 31 , 32 ]. Since age groups and gender were registered, we were able to identify the main diagnoses of sentinel hospital admissions in Madagascar for the different strata of the Malagasy population [ 33 ]. Nearly 50% of inpatients were registered by seven sentinel hospitals located in the central highlands, due to a higher population density in this area. In general, the majority of hospitalizations were due to acute medical and obstetric conditions. The sex ratio male / female was 0.66, with females being more prevalent due to maternity, as pregnant women represented 20.5% of the inpatients admitted into sentinel hospitals. Although, Madagascar was improving coverage of essential care, 70.7% of the inpatients were from the community directly. This situation explained by limited attendance at health facilities due to low household incomes and the poor quality of care provided in these facilities. Indeed, the outpatient consultation rate was 32.6%, and the rate of population living more than 10 km from a health facility was 35% [ 34 ]. Overall the web-based platform (case by case reporting) had 38.9% less entries than the routine HIS (an aggregated data reporting). Indeed, hospital managers in charge of data entry encountered difficulties to report case by case in relation to (i) the internet flow low or disturbance, (ii) backlog of registries to enter, (iii) assignment of trained staff, and (iv) wear and tear of the material. The reasons for hospitalization were recorded as free text in the database. This situation has led difficult to explore the data and may alter the quality of the results of the data analysis. Therefore, the coding of the reasons for hospitalization according to the ICD-10 is necessary. Given the large amount of data, classification must be done using automated techniques. The tool that was developed as a component of this study has helped in the coding of diagnoses in ICD-10. In Madagascar, the use of coding is uncommon. This system demonstrated that the classification of diagnosis using ICD-10 is necessary to provide factual and prompt data to improve the public health in Madagascar. This study showed that many patients with communicable and non-communicable diseases were potentially avoidable hospitalizations. The study results indicate a lack of preventive measures to health-care access and ambulatory care provided in a timely and effective manner. Limitations of the Study The sentinel surveillance data are used as direct estimate of the clinical burden of diseases in all low income countries [ 31 , 35 ]. However, several limitations are inherent of this approach. Hospital access is limited for rural communities because long and expensive travels will prevent many sick persons from visiting a hospital, especially since medical care is very expensive for the rural self-sufficient population, which is hardly insured. Therefore, the external validity (representativeness) and internal validity (selection bias) of these results should be considered. This sentinel hospital-based network has also several limitations regarding data quality. The incompleteness of the sentinel hospital data was 38.9%. This incompleteness is linked to the challenge of surveillance data collection, such as the technical processes (instability of internet), limitation of available computers for data input, and the field team members’ compliance to standard operating procedure (SOP). In addition, this study did not include outpatient departments in the 18 hospitals. The lack of inclusion of outpatients who consult at hospital centers may underevaluation the burden of various diseases in the country. Madagascar has various bioclimate zones, which were taken into account in the study by stratifying incidence by bioclimate zones, as tropical humid zones in the East coast and the Western coast enhance different diseases than semi arid zone in the South. However, for a precise analysis of the influence of climate on diseases, climate data would need to be included in statistical models analysing the association between bioclimate and disease incidence. Individual patient information was collected at the time of admission and the same patient was counted as many times as he/she was admitted to a hospital during the study period. This may have led to an overestimation of chronic diseases. However, once the diagnosis was made and the therapy defined, patients may have only returned to the primary health service level (community-based health center “CSB”) and not to the hospital. Since patient information was collected at the time of admission, in some cases the diagnosis might have been incomplete or incorrect. However, in most cases, patients had been referred from a community-based health centers or a midwife with a suspect diagnosis and point of care test results were available at time of admission. Despite those limitations, the sentinel surveillance is a highly valuable source to understand the clinical burden of diseases in Madagascar [ 23 , 36 ]. Conclusion In Madagascar, a web-based form was used to collect medical and demographic information from hospitalized patients. Once information was entered into the e-Health database, it was accessible and could be analyzed, which is useful for hospital planning and on a wider scale to define national public health policies. The pooling of epidemiological and clinical data is original and very useful for surveillance and for analyses of disease trends. Diseases of the cardio-vascular system had a high prevalence compared to other diseases in adults aged ≥ 15 years old and for all regions. Communicable diseases were the most frequent reason for hospitalization in the Western region and for children < 15 years. Malaria diagnosis was frequent in hospitalized children > 5 years, indicating a lack of proper malaria control at the community-based health care level in children older than five years old. The pregnant women represented 20.4% of the inpatients with the main reason of dystocic delivery. Madagascar is a low-income country and the access to health care varies by region and urbanization. The health financing model is based on patient co-payment for hospital care because many persons do not have a health care insurance. In this context, physicians play a central role in efforts to contain costs and improve diagnoses. Rising health care costs have been fueled by increased prevalence of preventable chronic disease related to different risk factors and unhealthy behaviors. The sentinel surveillance network in the 18 hospitals identified the main clinical diagnoses in hospitalized patients, which enables the development of universal health coverage in Madagascar. Acknowledgements We are grateful to all those who participated in this study, including, in particular, Bruno Aimé Nomenjanahary, Stephan Randrianasolo, Mariah Michel, Andoniaina Rakotondramanana, Charles Emile Ramarokoto, Jean Marius Rakotondramanga from Epidemiology and Clinical Research Unit of the Institut Pasteur de Madagascar, for their technical and administrative assistance. We further thank all staff of the Ministry of Public Health, in particular the head of the 18 sentinel hospitals surveillance for their cooperation. We also thank Dr Voahirana Nirina Ernestine † and Dr Andriatsarafara Haingo (CHU Haute Matsiatra, Fianarantsoa), Dr Rakotofiringa Zo Veromanitra (CHRR SAVA, Sambava), Dr Randriamanantena Harijaona Augustin † (CHRR Taolagnaro) and Dr Razafimanjato Andriniaina Rolland Ralph (CHU Analakinina, Toamasina). We would like to express our sincere thanks and gratitude to USAID technical team in particular Sixte Zigurumugabe, Judith Hedge, Laurent Kapesa, Catherine Dintenger and Jocelyn Razafindrakoto, for their collaboration. Abbreviations AIDS Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome CHRD District referent hospitals (“Centre Hospitalier de Référence du District”) CHRR Regional referent hospitals (“Centre Hospitalier de Référence Régionale”) CHU University-based hospitals (“Centre Hospitalier Universitaire”) CSB Community-based health centers (“Centre de Santé de Base”) HIS Health information system HIV Human immunodeficiency virus ICD-10 International statistical classification of diseases and related health problems, 10th revision IPM “Institut Pasteur de Madagascar” IQR Inter quartile range MDR-TB Multidrug resistant tuberculosis MoH Ministry of public health TB Tuberculosis WHO World health organization Authors Contribution L Randrianasolo (LR), LB Randriamampionona (LBR), M Randria (MiR), M Ratsitorahina (MaR) and P Piola (PP) conceived and designed the network hospitals sentinel surveillance. R Razanatsimba (RoR), H A Randrimbitsialonina (HAR), H Razafindrazaka (HR), J S Rafaliarisoa (JSR), N Ramanantoanina (NiR), H Randrianary (HaR), S Rakotonimanana (SoR), G Ranarijaona (GR), D Giovanie (DG), R Rasoamirantsoa (RaR), J Rasolofoharizanany (JaR), I Ravaonandrasana (IhR), A D Radama (ADR), L Fanambinantsoa (LF), V Ralijaona (VR), P Randrianasolo (PR), Randrianavelo (Ra), J Ralaivao (JR), N Rakotoavao (NR), T N Amaïde (TNA), A O Randrianantenaina (AOR), J E Botoihely (JEB), S Pierre (SP), T A B Zafilahy (TAZ), C M Andrianantenaina (CM) led data collection and data management at the sentinel hospital level. T Rabehasimbola (ToR), T Ramarokoto (THR), B Barivola (BB), FM Rakotoarimanana (FMR) and L Baril (LB) designed an automatic ICD-10 transcoding. L Randrianasolo (LR), A Dreyfus (AD), R Randremanana (RVR) and L Baril (LB) conducted all analyses and drafted the manuscript, with significant input. All authors have read, reviewed and approved the final manuscript. Funding This work was supported by the US Agency for International Development (USAID) by Surveillance and Data for Management (SDM) Project (Grant No. AID-687-G-13–00003). The funder had no role in study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, or preparation of the manuscript. The findings and conclusions in this report are those of the authors and do not necessarily represent the official position of US Agency for International Development or the Institut Pasteur de Madagascar. Data Availability The datasets generated and/or analyzed during the current study are the property of the Ministry of Public Health in Madagascar. Declarations Conflict of Interest The authors declare no competing interests. Ethical Approval and Consent to Participate Data were collected within the activities of the national public health surveillance systems in Madagascar. All methods were carried out in accordance with relevant guidelines and regulations of the Ethical Review Committee of Biomedical Research in Madagascar. Anonymized data was collected using a line list developed for the hospitalization registration. The line list was available in the hospital centers. The patient''s information was recorded on the line list. The use of the data collected in patients hospitalized in these hospitals participating in sentinel surveillance was exempted from ethical review of human subject research by the Ethical Review Committee of Biomedical Research in Madagascar (letter N° 111MSANP/SG/AMM/CERBM on May 26th, 2023). Consent for Publication A