. 10.1093/inthealth/ihaa036 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Xu Y, Chen X, Wang K. Global prevalence of hypertension among people living with HIV: a systematic review and meta-analysis. J Am Soc Hypertens. (2017) 11:530–40. 10.1016/j.jash.2017.06.004 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Fahme SA, Bloomfield GS, Peck R. Hypertension in HIV-infected adults: novel pathophysiologic mechanisms. Hypertension. (2018) 72:44–55. 10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.118.10893 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Chang AY, Gómez-Olivé FX, Payne C, Rohr JK, Manne-Goehler J, Wade AN, et al. Chronic multimorbidity among older adults in rural South Africa. BMJ Glob Health. (2019) 4:e001386. 10.1136/bmjgh-2018-001386 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Yoon H, Lee JH, Kim GS, Kim YJ, Hwang EY, Park CE, et al. The relationship between anemia and pulse pressure and hypertension: the Korea national health and nutrition examination survey 2010–2012. Clin Exp Hypertens. (2018) 40:650–5. 10.1080/10641963.2017.1416123 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Cheung BMY, Li C. Diabetes and hypertension: is there a common metabolic pathway? Curr Atheroscl Rep. (2012) 14:160–6. 10.1007/s11883-012-0227-2 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Bayes-Marin I, Sanchez-Niubo A, Egea-Cortés L, Nguyen H, Prina M, Fernández D, et al. Multimorbidity patterns in low-middle and high income regions: a multiregion latent class analysis using athlos harmonised cohorts. BMJ Open. (2020) 10:e034441. 10.1136/bmjopen-2019-034441 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Ndinda C, Ndhlovu TP, Juma P, Asiki G, Kyobutungi C. The evolution of non-communicable diseases policies in post-apartheid South Africa. BMC Public Health. (2018) 18:956. 10.1186/s12889-018-5832-8 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availab